.Dd September 23, 2026
.Dt CREATEHOMEDIR 8
.Os NextBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm createhomedir
.Nd create home directories
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl bcs
.Op Fl nv
.Fl a
.Nm
.Op Fl bcs
.Op Fl nv
.Fl u Ar user Op Fl u Ar user ...
.Nm
.Op Fl bcs
.Op Fl nv
.Fl i
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
creates a user's home directory, at mode 0755 owned by the user, and nothing
inside it.
.Pp
Home directories are normally created when the account is, by
.Xr adduser 8
or
.Xr pw 8 ,
and that covers network accounts too, because a network user is created on
the directory server, where the home lives and is exported to clients.
.Nm
therefore covers the rest: a home missing because a plist was restored,
hand-edited or copied between machines, and a home for an account created by
something other than
.Xr adduser 8 ,
such as Gershwin's
.Nm dscli ,
which writes the property lists directly.
.Xr login 1
degrades rather than failing in that case, reporting
.Dq No home directory. Logging in with home = / ,
so an affected user is not locked out and
.Nm
can be run afterwards.
.Pp
Users are enumerated with
.Xr getpwent 3 ,
so accounts are read through
.Xr nsswitch.conf 5
and any name source is treated alike.
Only homes under
.Pa /Local/Users
or
.Pa /Network/Users
are considered; system accounts in
.Xr master.passwd 5
point at
.Pa /var/empty ,
.Pa /nonexistent
and similar paths, which must stay absent.
An account whose id is 999 or below is refused whatever its home field says.
.Pp
An existing home is left alone.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl a
Create homes for every user the system can enumerate.
This is the default when none of
.Fl a , u
or
.Fl i
is given.
.It Fl b
Consider both local and network homes.
This is the default.
.It Fl c
Consider only homes under
.Pa /Local/Users .
.It Fl i
Read user names from standard input, one per line.
.It Fl n
Dry run: report what would be done and change nothing.
This differs from Darwin, where
.Fl n
names a directory domain; NextBSD has no equivalent to select.
.It Fl s
Consider only homes under
.Pa /Network/Users .
.It Fl P
Create the home for the user named by the
.Ev PAM_USER
environment variable, for use from
.Xr pam_exec 8
in a session.
Does nothing and succeeds when
.Ev PAM_USER
is unset.
.It Fl u Ar user
Create the home for
.Ar user .
May be repeated.
.It Fl v
Report each path as it is created.
.El
.Sh AT LOGIN
An account can exist without a home: Gershwin's
.Nm dscli
writes the property lists directly, and a restored plist can name a home that
is not there.
So
.Pa /etc/pam.d/login
and
.Pa /etc/pam.d/sshd
run
.Nm
in the session phase:
.Pp
.Dl session optional pam_exec.so /usr/sbin/createhomedir -P
.Pp
It is
.Cm optional
deliberately.
A home that cannot be created is not a reason to refuse someone a shell \(em
.Xr login 1
already degrades to
.Pa /
and says so.
.Pp
.Xr pam_exec 8
cannot substitute the user into an argument, which is why
.Fl P
reads
.Ev PAM_USER
rather than taking a name.
.Sh WHAT IS NOT CREATED
.Nm
creates the home directory and stops.
It does not create
.Pa Desktop ,
.Pa Documents ,
.Pa Downloads
or the rest: Gershwin's Workspace creates the set it wants at startup, and a
second set shipped here only produced two of some and none of others.
.Pp
No dot files are placed either.
.Pa /etc/zprofile
and the global zshrc mean an account needs none of its own, and
.Xr zsh 1
is not prompted to write one.
.Pp
Earlier versions copied a tree from
.Pa /System/Library/User Template
and applied an
.Pa __permissions.plist .
Both are gone.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/etc/pam.d/login" -compact
.It Pa /Local/Users
where a local home is created
.It Pa /Network/Users
where a network home is created
.It Pa /etc/pam.d/login
runs this at console login
.It Pa /etc/pam.d/sshd
runs this at ssh login
.It Pa /usr/share/skel
empty, as on Darwin
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
Repair every missing home:
.Pp
.Dl # createhomedir -a -v
.Pp
See what would be created for one user, without changing anything:
.Pp
.Dl $ createhomedir -n -v -u jane
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr nsswitch.conf 5 ,
.Xr adduser 8 ,
.Xr pw 8
.Sh HISTORY
A
.Nm
command first appeared in
Mac OS X, in section 1 and driven by Open Directory domains.
The
.Nm
described here was written for NextBSD and reads accounts through the
name-service switch instead.
.Sh CAVEATS
.Nm
does not create the account itself, and will not invent a home for a user the
system cannot resolve.
